          "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Matthew 15:24 explicitly identifies the lost sheep as the house of Israel. The prophetic material supplied with the entry depicts Ephraim/Israel as mixed among the peoples, deprived of national identity, sought by God, and ultimately reunited and restored. The broader application to an unrepentant sinner is supported by Luke 15:3-7, where the lost sheep is expressly interpreted as a sinner who repents. The strongest counter-reading—spiritually straying covenant Israel in Jesus' immediate ministry, without a specific emphasis on dispersed Ephraim—does not deny the entry's core referent and is less comprehensive rather than contradictory.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The lost sheep are the house of Israel, understood in the supplied prophetic framework as scattered Israel whom God seeks and regathers.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The scattered house of Israel became mingled with the nations.\",\n    \"The lost are sought and regathered.\",\n    \"More generally, a lost sheep may represent any unrepentant sinner.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The identification of Jacob as the universal crooked human heart and Israel as a covenant identity wholly independent of descent is substantially broader than the glossary entry and rests on debatable typological steps.\",\n    \"The equation of Jacob's blessing concerning a multitude of nations with Paul's 'fulness of the Gentiles' is suggestive but not textually demonstrated by the shared English formulation alone.\",\n    \"The identification of Zechariah's and Revelation's two olive trees as two corporate covenant peoples rather than two individuals is not necessary to establish the glossary entry.\",\n    \"The chapter's claim that Ephraim was deliberately lost as seed in every nation goes beyond the limited assertion that Israel was scattered and mingled among nations.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"In Matthew 15:24, the lost sheep are covenant Israel generally—particularly the spiritually straying and pastorally neglected Jews who constituted the immediate scope of Jesus' earthly mission—not specifically dispersed Ephraim or Israelites assimilated among Gentile nations. In the parables, the image broadens to individual sinners or wandering disciples.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"Spiritually straying covenant Israel in the immediate Matthean setting, with individual sinners or disciples as the referent in other contexts.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter does not materially deny that the Matthean lost sheep are the house of Israel; it chiefly resists making dispersed, nation-mingled Israel the exclusive or necessary sense.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Matthew 15:24 itself says 'the lost sheep of the house of Israel' but does not explicitly mention exile, Ephraim, assimilation, or regathering.\",\n    \"The immediate contrast with the Canaanite woman naturally distinguishes Israel from Gentiles, which can favor covenant Israel generally rather than Israelites already mingled among the nations.\",\n    \"Matthew 18 and Luke 15 apply the sheep image contextually to a wandering little one or a repentant sinner, showing that the symbol is not uniformly restricted to national restoration.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"It takes Matthew 15:24's explicit identification with the house of Israel at full force.\",\n    \"It integrates that identification with the established prophetic pattern of Israel as scattered sheep whom God seeks and restores.\",\n    \"Hosea's statement that Ephraim 'mixed himself among the people' supports the entry's nation-mingling language.\",\n    \"Ezekiel's reunification of Judah and Joseph-Ephraim supports the regathering component.\",\n    \"Its general-sinner qualification accommodates Luke 15 rather than forcing every occurrence into an exclusively national sense.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"It stays closer to the immediate wording of Matthew 15:24 and requires fewer steps from that verse alone.\",\n    \"It readily explains why Jesus initially contrasts his mission to Israel with the request of a Canaanite woman.\",\n    \"It preserves contextual variation between Matthew 15, Matthew 18, and Luke 15.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Matthew 15:24\",\n    \"Hosea 7:8\",\n    \"Ezekiel 37:16-22\",\n    \"Jeremiah 50:6\",\n    \"Ezekiel 34:11-16\",\n    \"Luke 15:3-7\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"Matthew 15:24 alone does not prove that Jesus intended the narrower Ephraim-diaspora framework rather than covenant Israel generally.\",\n    \"The precise relationship between dispersed Ephraim and the people addressed during Jesus' earthly ministry remains inferential.\",\n    \"The symbol's scope plainly varies by context, so the national identification should not be imposed uniformly on every lost-sheep passage.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n}"
        }
